\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{When effective weights admit representation expansions}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Suppose the reference of an
\vocabk{effective gauge weight}{fdip-00MX} is product Haar on
#{G^{E_c}}, with #{G} compact Lie, and suppose
#{f\in L^2(G^{E_c})}. For a unitary irreducible
#{\rho:G\to U(V_\rho)}, let #{\mathcal C_\rho\subset L^2(G)}
be its finite-dimensional coefficient space, consisting of
linear combinations of #{g\mapsto\lambda(\rho(g)v)} with
#{\lambda\in V_\rho^*} and #{v\in V_\rho}.
For an edge-representation tuple
#{\boldsymbol\rho=(\rho_e)_{e\in E_c}}, put
##{\mathcal C_{\boldsymbol\rho}
 =\bigotimes_{e\in E_c}\mathcal C_{\rho_e}
 \ \subset L^2(G^{E_c}),\qquad
 f_{\boldsymbol\rho}=\Pi_{\boldsymbol\rho}f,}
where #{\Pi_{\boldsymbol\rho}} is orthogonal projection.
Then
##{f=\sum_{\boldsymbol\rho}f_{\boldsymbol\rho}}
converges in #{L^2}, as the limit over finite sets of tuples.
If additionally
#{\sum_{\boldsymbol\rho}\|f_{\boldsymbol\rho}\|_\infty<\infty},
the series converges uniformly absolutely to a continuous
representative of #{f}. The sup norm here uses the canonical
continuous representative of each coefficient-space element.}

\p{If #{f} is gauge invariant, each
#{f_{\boldsymbol\rho}} is gauge invariant. Under the intrinsic
coefficient identification
#{\mathcal C_{\rho_e}\cong V_{\rho_e}^*\otimes V_{\rho_e}},
regrouping factors by vertices identifies the invariant
coefficient space with the tensor product of the invariant
parts of
##{W_v=
 \left(\bigotimes_{e:t(e)=v}V_{\rho_e}^*\right)
 \otimes
 \left(\bigotimes_{e:s(e)=v}V_{\rho_e}\right).}
The gauge action on each dual factor is contragredient;
on each undualized factor it is the given representation.
A loop contributes both factors at the same vertex.
Thus the surviving functions are vertex-invariant tensor
contractions. For a central density on one copy of #{G},
the specialization is the character expansion and
convergence criterion in \ref{fdip-007C}.}

\proof{
\p{Peter–Weyl in \ref{fdip-007C} gives the orthogonal
decomposition #{L^2(G)=\widehat{\bigoplus}_\rho\mathcal C_\rho}.
Tensoring over the finite edge set proves the tuple
decomposition and the asserted #{L^2} sum. Coefficient
functions are continuous. Haar has full support, so their
continuous representatives are unique.
The extra sup-norm summability assumption gives a uniformly
absolutely convergent series of these continuous functions.
Its limit agrees with #{f} in #{L^2}.}

\p{For one edge, inverse gauge pullback sends the coefficient
#{\lambda(\rho_e(u_e)v)} to
##{\lambda\bigl(\rho_e(g_{t(e)}^{-1})
                 \rho_e(u_e)\rho_e(g_{s(e)})v\bigr).}
Thus the coefficient tensor transforms as
#{\lambda\mapsto\lambda\circ\rho_e(g_{t(e)}^{-1})}
at the target and #{v\mapsto\rho_e(g_{s(e)})v}
at the source. This proves the stated vertex actions
and preservation of every tuple space. The action is
unitary on #{L^2}, so it preserves the orthogonal complement
of each tuple space as well. Hence it commutes with
#{\Pi_{\boldsymbol\rho}}, and each component of an invariant
#{f} is invariant.}

\p{The gauge group is the product of the vertex groups.
After regrouping the coefficient factors, its Haar
projection is the tensor product of the projections
onto #{W_v^G}. Each is the finite-dimensional invariant
projection supplied by \ref{fdip-0083}. Its image is
#{\bigotimes_v W_v^G}, giving the invariant contractions.
No absolute convergence follows from #{L^2} membership
alone.}
}

\p{For a general image reference, such as the diagonal law
of \ref{fdip-00MS}, these product-Haar coefficient spaces
need not be mutually orthogonal. An arbitrary #{L^1}
effective density also need not be in #{L^2}. The exact
observable and composition identities of \ref{fdip-00MQ}
still apply in both cases.}
